# NOVA Profile Core -- Chromium patch series.
#
# Apply to a Chromium checkout with:
#
#     git apply novaprofile/core_patch/patches/*.patch
#
# The series touches five Blink files.  Every change is a *substitution* of
# an existing accessor with a config-backed accessor; no new DOM property
# is introduced, so a page cannot detect the patch by enumerating the
# prototype.
#
# The client hints are patched as well as the User-Agent: the brand list
# gives the browser build away and the high-entropy values give the device
# away, so a profile that patched only the User-Agent would contradict
# itself in the very first request a page makes.
#
# Every substituted accessor keeps the stock behaviour as its fallback: if
# the profile configuration has not been loaded -- which is the case in the
# browser's own pages and in a crash handler -- the original value is
# returned unchanged.  A patch that replaced the fallback would make the
# browser itself inconsistent.
#
# The configuration is loaded lazily by the hooks themselves
# (``NovaConfigHolder::EnsureInitialized``), so the patch does not have to
# touch the process start-up code: a renderer or the GPU process that
# reaches an accessor before the browser's own initialization has run
# still reports the profile's values, never the host's.  That keeps the
# diff against the Chromium tree small and independent of the line numbers
# of the upstream sources.
#
# The reference implementation of these hooks lives in
# ``novaprofile/core_patch/src/nova_blink_patch.cc``; the two must be kept
# in step, and ``tests/test_core_patch_coverage.py`` enforces that every
# accessor substituted here is also implemented there.
#
# The context lines of every hunk are stock Chromium code, so the series
# applies cleanly against an unmodified tree.

